    "title": "What time is the Artemis 2 splashdown, and where to livestream it",
    "text": "After a successful trip around the moon, Artemis II crew members are preparing for their return home.Their 10-day mission first began April 1 with four astronauts taking the journey into space. Crew members are now making their way back to Earth, with their return set for today, April 10.South Jersey headlines: Why a small South Jersey town has been rated a destination for foodiesWhere is Artemis 2 now?As of late morning April 10, Artemis II was slightly more than 60,000 miles from Earth and approximately 191,000 miles from the moon.For the most up-to-date information on Artemis II’s location, NASA has an online tracker that allows users to see where the ship is, how fast it’s traveling and how far it is from Earth and the moon.The crew of Artemis II captured a breathtaking image of a celestial event known as an \"Earthset,\" in which the Earth dropped below the lunar horizon. The image is reminiscent of the iconic \"Earthrise\" photo that NASA astronaut Bill Anders captured in 1968 during the Apollo 8 mission that showed our planet rising on the lunar horizon.What time is Artemis 2 splashdown?Artemis II is set to officially return to Earth on Friday evening.Splashdown is scheduled to occur at approximately 8:07 p.m. Eastern Stanard Time, according to NASA. The vessel is to land in the Pacific Ocean, off the coast of San Diego, California.This map from NASA displays where the Orion spacecraft will land off the coast of California, bringing to an end the Artemis II mission.Artemis 2 splashdown livestreamUSA TODAY has planned a livestream of the Artemis II splashdown on YouTube.In addition to many major TV networks carrying splashdown coverage, it may also be livestreamed via:Risha Inaganti writes about trending topics across South Jersey for the Courier-Post. If you have a story she should tell, email her at rinaganti@usatodayco.com. Subscribe to stay up to date on the news you need.This article originally appeared on Cherry Hill Courier-Post: Artemis 2 splashdown is planned today in the ocean near San Diego",
